WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.547
Building your first PageMotor plugin. One folder, one PHP file.

2
00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:17.200
You declare a class, opt into the capabilities you need, activate it, and PageMotor does the rest. This takes you from an empty folder to a working shortcode.

3
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.362
The whole mental model first, because once you have it the rest is mechanical.

4
00:00:23.362 --> 00:00:34.215
A plugin is a folder under user hyphen content, plugins, with a plugin dot p h p inside it. That file declares a PHP class extending P M underscore Plugin.

5
00:00:34.215 --> 00:00:45.278
And you add capabilities by implementing valet methods: named methods that PageMotor calls at specific moments. Implement shortcodes, and you have shortcodes.

6
00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:54.031
Implement nothing at all, and you have a perfectly valid plugin that simply does nothing. You opt in, rather than hooking in.

7
00:00:54.731 --> 00:01:03.746
Step one, the folder and the header. Make your folder under user hyphen content, plugins, and create plugin dot p h p inside it.

8
00:01:03.746 --> 00:01:14.381
Name the folder in kebab case, and start it with a prefix unique to you or your organisation, so it cannot collide with anything else in the ecosystem.

9
00:01:14.381 --> 00:01:26.496
Then open that file and add the header comment. PageMotor reads that comment to identify the plugin, so it must sit at the very top of the file, before any PHP code at all.

10
00:01:27.196 --> 00:01:37.770
Here is the first thing that catches people, and it is an odd one. PageMotor scans only the opening five hundred bytes of plugin dot p h p looking for the Version header.

11
00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:47.225
Push that line below a long description block, and the version badge simply never appears. Nothing errors, nothing warns you, it is just quietly absent.

12
00:01:47.225 --> 00:01:51.019
So keep the header short, and put Version near the top of it.

13
00:01:51.719 --> 00:02:00.003
Step two, the class. Directly below the header comment, declare a class that extends P M underscore Plugin. That is it.

14
00:02:00.003 --> 00:02:07.590
Those two lines are a complete, valid plugin. It does nothing yet, but PageMotor can load it and activate it.

15
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:22.695
One thing to be careful about: the class name must match the Class field in the header exactly. PageMotor instantiates the class by reading that header, so a typo in either place means the plugin simply will not load.

16
00:02:23.395 --> 00:02:28.540
The base class gives you two override slots for your own start-up code.

17
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:38.324
Construct runs early, before any valets are evaluated: use it for database setup, includes, anything that has to exist before the rest.

18
00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:48.180
And init runs after all the valets have been registered: use that for work which depends on settings or options already being available.

19
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:58.689
There are also two you must leave alone. Double underscore construct, and underscore init, are framework-owned. Those two are not yours to touch.

20
00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:13.530
Step three, adding an actual capability. To register a shortcode, implement the shortcodes valet. It returns an array mapping shortcode names to method names on your own class.

21
00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:21.002
Then write the handler. It receives any attributes as an args array, and it returns its HTML.

22
00:03:21.002 --> 00:03:32.733
Note that word: returns. It does not echo. Get that the wrong way round and your output will appear in a strange place on the page, or not at all.

23
00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:46.584
Now the second thing that catches people, and it is worth knowing before it costs you an afternoon. If your shortcode needs its own CSS, register it with c s s underscore sitewide, not plain c s s.

24
00:03:46.584 --> 00:03:56.664
The plain one only loads when an instance of the plugin has been placed in the current page template, which is exactly right for Blocks and Containers.

25
00:03:56.664 --> 00:04:06.544
But a shortcode can appear on any page at all. So its styles have to be sitewide, or they will be missing precisely where you did not think to test.

26
00:04:07.244 --> 00:04:15.078
Steps four and five. In the admin, go to Plugins. Yours appears in the list under the Name from your header.

27
00:04:15.078 --> 00:04:29.005
Click Install, which registers it in the database, then click Activate, which instantiates the class and evaluates its valets on every page load. Uploading the folder on its own is not enough.

28
00:04:29.005 --> 00:04:33.575
Then edit any page, drop the shortcode into the body, and save.

29
00:04:33.575 --> 00:04:47.647
One caveat worth knowing: shortcodes only run in page content, not in a page served as verbatim HTML. If your output does not appear, check the page type before you go looking through your code.

30
00:04:48.347 --> 00:04:55.631
And one last thing, which is the reason to keep early plugins simple. PageMotor includes your plugin file directly.

31
00:04:55.631 --> 00:05:04.498
So a PHP parse error, or an uncaught fatal, will stop every page on the site from loading until that file is fixed or the plugin is removed.

32
00:05:04.498 --> 00:05:13.239
Work with a safety copy, test each change as you make it, and if something breaks, fix the file on the server before you do anything else.

